---
title: Page data
summary: $page.data (your load data, plus url, params, route, status, form and error) can be read inside islands, and a load that returns a promise streams into the island live.
related:
  - data-state/remote-functions
  - data-state/state-context
  - regions/client-islands
---

An island often needs the page's own data, such as the current user, the route params or the result of a `form` action, and threading it down as props would be tedious. Instead, an island reads `$page.data` and the rest of Kit's page object exactly as a normal Kit component does. Your `load` runs on the server as always, and its data reaches every island on the page. For data an island fetches _itself_, on demand or after a click, use a [remote function](/docs/data-state/remote-functions) instead.

```svelte title=Greeting.svelte
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
</script>

<p>Bonjour, {page.data.user.name} — you are on {page.url.pathname}</p>
```

The whole page object crosses the boundary: `data`, `params`, `url`, `route`, `status`, `form` and `error`. It is the same data your `load` returns, treated as visible to the client, which is the same contract as on a `csr = true` Kit page.

## Streaming promises

Kit lets `load` return a **promise** at any level of `data`, so the page goes out right away and the value follows when it resolves. On a normal Kit page the browser streams it in. A `csr = false` page has no page-level hydration, so Kit's own stream does nothing there, but an **island has a client**, and ogygia streams the promise into the island.

```ts title=+page.server.ts
export const load = () => ({
  country: 'FR',                                   // sent immediately
  slow: fetch('/api/recommendations').then((r) => r.json())  // a promise — streamed
});
```

```svelte title=Recs.svelte
<script>
  import { page } from '$app/state';
</script>

{#await page.data.slow}
  <p>Loading recommendations…</p>
{:then recs}
  <ul>{#each recs as r}<li>{r.title}</li>{/each}</ul>
{/await}
```

The page shell and the island's `Loading…` branch **paint immediately**, so the first paint never waits for the slowest promise. Each promise resolves on its own as it settles, fast ones first, and the island's `{#await}` switches to its result live, with no extra request.

A promise that **rejects** streams as an error and shows the `{:catch}` branch. A promise that resolves to a value containing **more promises** streams those too, recursively, as in Kit.

## Custom types

Types you teach Kit through the [`transport`](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/hooks#Universal-hooks-transport) hook arrive in islands as real instances, both in plain data and inside a streamed promise, so you are not limited to built-in types like `Date` and `Map`.

```ts title=src/hooks.ts
import * as ogygia from 'ogygia';

export class Money {
  constructor(public cents: number) {}
  get pretty() { return `$${(this.cents / 100).toFixed(2)}`; }
}

export const transport = {
  ...ogygia.transport,
  Money: { encode: (v) => v instanceof Money && [v.cents], decode: ([c]) => new Money(c) }
};
```

`page.data.price.pretty` then works inside the island. The getter runs because `decode` rebuilt the class rather than a plain copy.

## Notes

- **A component shared with a `csr = true` page** reads the correct page object on both kinds of page. On an island page the seed feeds it, and on a Kit-hydrated page ogygia passes Kit's own live `page` through to it, so reading `page.data` in a shared header or footer is safe everywhere without guards.
- **A `form` action result** (`page.form`) and `page.error` reach islands the same way, so an island can render the outcome of a `<form method="POST">` without a prop.
- **Client navigation** with the [SPA router](/docs/app/spa-router) cannot run streamed scripts in the middle of a swap, so on those loads a promise is awaited on the server and handed to the island already settled. `page.data.x` is still a promise you `{#await}`, so your island code is the same in both cases.
- **Treat load data as visible to the client**, exactly as on a `csr = true` page, because it is serialized into the HTML. Don't put anything in `load` data that you wouldn't send to the browser.
- A **server island** (`render: 'deferred'`) renders on its own endpoint, yet its components still read the page they are mounted on: ogygia looks that page up through Kit's own data request, and the answer carries the `page.data` keys the hole's islands read (see [A lake that can refresh](/docs/regions/held-regions#a-lake-that-can-refresh)).
- **The seed is shaped to what is read.** The build reads every module that imports `$app/state` or `$app/stores` and records the top-level `page.data` keys it reaches, whether written as `page.data.x`, `$page?.data?.x`, `page.data['x']` or `const { x } = page.data`, including through aliases like `const v = get(page)` or `derived(page, ($p) => …)`. A page then ships only the keys its islands read, plus any node an island's props point into. A read the build cannot pin to a literal key ships everything, as before; that includes passing `page.data` to a function, aliasing it whole, spreading it, indexing it with a variable, calling `page.subscribe(…)` or passing on the whole `page`. So on a page whose load returns a large tree for the server render, read literal keys in islands to keep the seed small. Whenever a read forces the whole tree to ship, the build names the file and line.
